Below is a quick‑reference guide to every practical way you can drop a **geocoder search box** onto a **Mapbox GL JS v2** map.  
I’ve grouped the options by “plug‑and‑play” (official or community controls) vs. “DIY” (call the API yourself) and added the key trade‑offs, code snippets, and links so you can pick the one that fits your project’s timeline, budget, and licensing needs.

---

## 1️⃣ Official, ready‑made controls

| Option | What it is | How you add it | When to use it |
|--------|------------|----------------|----------------|
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`** (Mapbox GL Geocoder) | A small, self‑contained UI control that talks to the Mapbox Geocoding API. Works out‑of‑the‑box with Mapbox GL JS v2. | `npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`  <br>or load from CDN. | You want the classic autocomplete dropdown, biasing, language support, and a single‑line integration. |
| **Mapbox Search UI (`@mapbox/search-js` + `@mapbox/search-ui`)** | A richer “search box + results list” UI built on the newer **Mapbox Search API** (which adds relevance ranking, POI categories, and optional “search‑as‑you‑type” analytics). | `npm i @mapbox/search-js @mapbox/search-ui`  <br>or use the hosted script from `https://api.mapbox.com/search/v1/search.js`. | You need a more modern UI (cards, filters, “search results” pane) or want to use the **Search API** (which is separate from the classic Geocoding API and has a higher free‑tier). |
| **`MapboxGeocoder` as a React component (`react-map-gl-geocoder`)** | A thin wrapper that lets you drop the control into a React app that already uses `react-map-gl`. | `npm i react-map-gl-geocoder` | You’re building a React SPA and want the control to be a React component (state‑aware, easy to style). |
| **`MapboxGeocoder` for Vue (`vue-mapbox-geocoder`)** | Same idea as the React wrapper, but for Vue. | `npm i vue-mapbox-geocoder` | Vue‑centric projects. |

### 1‑A. Example – the classic `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Mapbox GL JS v2 + Geocoder</title>
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v5.0.1/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v5.0.1/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
    accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
    mapboxgl: mapboxgl,
    placeholder: 'Search for places',
    // optional biasing:
    proximity: { longitude: -122.447303, latitude: 37.753574 },
    // limit results, filter types, language, etc.
    types: 'place,postcode,address',
    language: 'en',
    limit: 5
  });

  // Add the control to the top‑left corner
  map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left');

  // Optional: listen for result events
  geocoder.on('result', e => console.log('Result:', e.result));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Why it’s popular**

* Zero‑config UI (dropdown, keyboard navigation, clear button).  
* Works with Mapbox GL v2 without any polyfills.  
* Supports all the classic Geocoding API parameters (`proximity`, `bbox`, `types`, `language`, `limit`).  
* Free for up to **100 k** geocoding requests per month (same quota as the Mapbox Maps API).  

---

## 2️⃣ The newer **Mapbox Search API** + UI

Mapbox introduced a **Search API** (v1) that is built on top of the **Pelias** open‑source engine but adds Mapbox‑specific ranking, POI categories, and optional analytics. It’s a separate product from the classic Geocoding API, with its own pricing tier (free up to **50 k** requests/month, then $0.75 per 1 k).

### 2‑A. Quick‑start UI (`@mapbox/search-ui`)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Mapbox Search UI + GL JS v2</title>
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/search/v1/search.js?access_token=YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/search/v1/search.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div id="search-container" style="position:absolute; top:10px; left:10px; width:300px;"></div>

<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';
  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 1️⃣ Initialise the Search client
  const searchClient = new MapboxSearch({ accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken });

  // 2️⃣ Initialise the UI component
  const searchUI = new MapboxSearchUI({
    container: '#search-container',
    client: searchClient,
    placeholder: 'Search places, POIs…',
    // optional: limit to a country or set a bounding box
    options: {
      countries: ['us'],
      bbox: [-123, 36, -121, 38]
    }
  });

  // 3️⃣ When a result is selected, fly to it on the map
  searchUI.on('result', (event) => {
    const { center, place_name } = event.result;
    map.flyTo({ center, zoom: 14 });
    // optional: add a marker
    new mapboxgl.Marker().setLngLat(center).addTo(map);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Pros**

* Modern UI (cards, images, category icons).  
* Built‑in **category filters** (`categories: ['cafe', 'restaurant']`).  
* Supports **fuzzy matching** and **relevance ranking** that is a bit more “POI‑aware” than the classic API.  
* Separate pricing tier (useful if you already hit the classic Geocoding quota).  

**Cons**

* Slightly larger bundle (≈ 30 KB gzipped) vs. the classic geocoder.  
* Not all legacy parameters (`proximity` works, but `bbox` is now `options.bbox`).  
* Requires a **Search API token** (same token works, but you must enable the Search product in your Mapbox account).  

---

## 3️⃣ DIY – Call the Geocoding / Search API yourself

If you need a **custom UI** (e.g., a modal, a sidebar with a table of results, or integration with a form library), you can skip the pre‑built control and hit the API directly.

### 3‑A. Classic Geocoding API (REST)

```js
async function forwardGeocode(query) {
  const url = new URL('https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/' + encodeURIComponent(query) + '.json');
  url.search = new URLSearchParams({
    access_token: mapboxgl.accessToken,
    limit: 5,
    proximity: '-122.447303,37.753574', // optional
    language: 'en',
    types: 'place,address,postcode'
  });

  const resp = await fetch(url);
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.features; // array of result objects
}
```

You can then render the results however you like (list, table, map markers, etc.).  

**When to go DIY**

* You need a UI that the official control can’t provide (e.g., a multi‑select, a “search‑and‑save” workflow, or integration with a design system).  
* You want to **debounce** requests differently, or you need to **cache** results locally.  
* You want to combine **multiple geocoding services** (Mapbox + a local dataset) in a single UI.

### 3‑B. Search API (REST)

```js
async function searchAPI(query) {
  const url = new URL('https://api.mapbox.com/search/v1/forward');
  url.search = new URLSearchParams({
    access_token: mapboxgl.accessToken,
    query,
    limit: 7,
    language: 'en',
    // optional: filter by category
    // categories: 'cafe,restaurant'
  });

  const resp = await fetch(url);
  const data = await resp.json();
  return data.features; // same shape as classic API
